If `app_label` is passed to `resolve_relation`, it is returned as-is. If the model is a string without a dot, `app_label` is returned as-is. If the model is a model class, `model._meta.app_label` is returned.

Wait, `model._meta.app_label` might not be lowercased?

In Django, `AppConfig.name` can be anything, but `AppConfig.label` is usually what's used as `app_label`.

If the app name is `MyApp`, then `AppConfig.label` might be `MyApp`.

Let's see what `model._meta.app_label` is for `MyApp`.

In my reproduction script, I didn't actually create a real Django app.

But in the issue description: "app with uppercase letters in the app name : django-admin startproject mysite; cd mysite; python manage.py startapp MyApp".

When you run `startapp MyApp`, Django creates an `apps.py` with:
```python
class MyAppConfig(AppConfig):
    default_auto_field = apps.AutoField
    name = 'MyApp'
```
In this case, `label` defaults to `name` if not specified. So `label` is `'MyApp'`.
Then `model._meta.app_label` will be `'MyApp'`.

